{
  "section": "llm-online-policy-optimization",
  "addedResources": [],
  "units": [
    {
      "id": "sequence-policy-gradient",
      "title": "On-policy generation and the sequence policy gradient",
      "hours": 4,
      "target": [
        "derive"
      ],
      "topics": [
        "rl-11-on-policy-prompt-sampling-and-completion-generation",
        "rl-11-sequence-policy-gradient-for-autoregressive-trajectories"
      ],
      "outcomes": [
        "Derive the sequence policy gradient from the autoregressive factorization with a fixed prompt distribution.",
        "Identify the distribution that actually generated a completion and the log probabilities its update requires."
      ],
      "why": "The sequence objective is familiar REINFORCE, but generation now owns the sampling distribution. A correct loss on text drawn from a different decoding policy is not an on-policy estimator.",
      "next": [
        {
          "unit": "reinforcement-learning/llm-online-policy-optimization/token-reinforce-terminal-rewards",
          "note": "Turn the sequence derivative into a masked token loss."
        }
      ],
      "study": [
        {
          "resource": "rl11-rlhf-book-policy-gradients-and-regularization",
          "title": "RLHF Book: sequence policy gradient",
          "url": "https://rlhfbook.com/c/06-policy-gradients.html#deriving-the-policy-gradient",
          "locator": "Chapter 6, The Role of Reinforcement Learning in RLHF and Deriving the Policy Gradient, equations 9 to 18 (page read 2026-10-14)"
        },
        {
          "resource": "rl11-rlhf-book-course-lectures-3-and-4",
          "title": "Lecture 3: RL Motivation and Math",
          "url": "https://rlhfbook.com/teach/course/lec3-chap6-p1/",
          "locator": "Slides 20 to 28: differentiating the expectation, trajectory factorization and the token-log-probability sum",
          "depth": "Build on the earlier likelihood-ratio identity and Decoding policies: from greedy to nucleus sampling. Skip the classical RL review before slide 20."
        }
      ],
      "focus": [
        "For a fixed prompt distribution and parameter-independent reward, \\(\\nabla_\\theta J = \\mathbb{E}[R(x,y)\\sum_t \\nabla_\\theta \\log \\pi_\\theta(y_t\\mid x,y_{<t})]\\). Include the sampled stopping action when EOS ends the response.",
        "The prompt distribution and deterministic append transition have zero parameter derivative. The completion distribution does not.",
        "Temperature and truncation change the policy, not just its presentation. Start with untruncated categorical sampling at temperature 1; otherwise define and score the transformed policy explicitly."
      ],
      "reproduce": "Closed-book, enumerate a two-token binary policy with independent tokens, each with probability \\(p\\) of 1, and reward 1 only for two ones. Derive \\(J(p)=p^2\\) and recover \\(dJ/dp=2p\\) from the expected score-function estimator. At \\(p=1/2\\), both give 1. This oracle needs only CPU arithmetic.",
      "traps": [
        "Backpropagating the mean of frozen verifier scores. It has no path to policy parameters; the score-function loss supplies that path.",
        "Calling a completion on-policy because its model weights match. Top-p filtering, temperature, dropout and numerical backend differences can still change its distribution."
      ],
      "selfTest": [
        {
          "q": "Why is there no explicit trajectory probability multiplying each sampled gradient?",
          "a": "Sampling already weights trajectories by their probability. Multiplying by that probability again estimates a different expectation."
        },
        {
          "q": "Does the sequence gradient require gradients through sampled token IDs?",
          "a": "No. Hold the sampled IDs fixed and differentiate their log probabilities under the policy."
        },
        {
          "q": "Can raw model log probabilities describe nucleus sampling exactly?",
          "a": "No. The sampler removes tokens and renormalizes the retained probabilities. Raw log probabilities omit that transformation and its support restriction."
        }
      ],
      "practice": [],
      "practiceNote": "The section mini-project covers this: the sequence-gradient oracle and the generation-policy contract in policylab's rollout collector.",
      "stopWhen": "Your enumeration matches the analytic derivative for several probabilities, and you can name the sampler and scoring distribution for every rollout.",
      "fit": {
        "verdict": "kept",
        "note": "Chapter 6 and Lecture 3 supply the derivation; the mini-project supplies an exact finite-policy oracle and sampling-contract practice."
      }
    },
